[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router_core_1, router_core_2, router_core_3, switch_dist_1_1, switch_dist_2_1, switch_server_access
diagnosis_text:
The OSPF control-plane (FRR/ospfd) across the campus fabric briefly reset, causing a transient loss of inter-building reachability. Evidence: all routed nodes now show OSPF-learned routes with very fresh installation timers (~1–2 minutes), indicating a near-simultaneous OSPF reconvergence. For example, on router_core_1 the OSPF route to 10.2.1.0/24 is [110/30] via 172.16.0.1, age 00:01:33; router_core_2 shows 10.1.1.0/24 [110/30] via 172.16.0.0, age 00:01:33; switch_server_access shows 10.1.1.0/24 and 10.2.1.0/24 via 172.16.0.10, age 00:01:33. Both distribution switches show directly connected SVIs for their local VLANs and remote subnets learned via OSPF with similar recent ages. 

Mechanism: while OSPF adjacencies were down/restarting, LSAs and inter-building prefixes were withdrawn, so hosts in one building could not reach anything in the other. Once OSPF sessions reformed, reachability returned. Current validation shows the fabric is healthy: host_1_1_1_1 and host_2_1_1_1 can ping each other and the server/DNS network with 0% loss, and HTTP to web servers completes in ~2 ms.

Localization: OSPF processes on all FRR routers (cores, both distribution switches, and the server access router) restarted nearly in lockstep.

Root cause: Near-simultaneous restart or flap of OSPF daemons across the routing fabric (maintenance or crash) caused a temporary isolation between buildings.

Corrective fix: Avoid coordinated control-plane restarts; stagger maintenance, ensure FRR/ospfd supervision and health checks, and add monitoring/alerts for OSPF neighbor flaps. No action needed now—service is restored; review FRR logs to identify the trigger. 
=== END ===